# Shell scripts must stay LF — they run via bash on the RunPod pod / Git Bash.
# CRLF line endings break `#!/bin/bash` and every command with a trailing \r.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Hash-pinned training artifacts must have identical bytes on Windows and Linux.
data/training/aria_tooluse_resolution_boundary_dpo_v1.jsonl text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe requires CRLF. With bare LF
# it mis-parses the file and fails with "'M' is not recognized as an internal or
# external command" (the tail of a REM line being read as a command) — observed
# 2026-08-03 after aria.cmd was rewritten by an LF-emitting editor.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# R-F4283 — HASH-PINNED EVIDENCE, as a CLASS rather than one file at a time.
#
# Eval reports and training corpora are pinned by sha256 in verdicts, manifests
# and launchers. Git normalises them to LF in the blob while a Windows checkout
# writes CRLF, so `git status` reads CLEAN while the bytes on disk hash
# differently from the bytes every recorded sha was taken over. Measured
# 2026-08-24: 154 of 239 tracked files under these directories were mangled, and
# `test_rf4153` failed on exactly that — a false failure that names no defect and
# cannot reproduce in CI, which is the worst kind.
#
# The single-file line above (resolution_boundary_dpo_v1) was this same defect,
# fixed for one instance. This covers the class.
data/eval_reports/**  text eol=lf
data/training/**      text eol=lf

# ...but NEVER line-ending-convert a binary under those trees. `text eol=lf` above
# would mark a 310MB `.tgz` adapter as text and corrupt it on checkout. Today the
# checkpoints are untracked so nothing is at risk; this makes sure that stays true
# if one is ever committed.
data/training/**/*.tgz       -text
data/training/**/*.gz        -text
data/training/**/*.tar       -text
data/training/**/*.safetensors -text
data/training/**/*.bin       -text
data/eval_reports/**/*.tgz   -text
data/eval_reports/**/*.gz    -text
